# A generic circuit breaker: watch any rule, at a threshold of its own,
# without editing the rule being watched -- and the suspension is
# TEMPORARY, not a permanent kill. A rule dormant forever is starved of its
# own future legitimate work; the point is to stop it from starving
# EVERYTHING ELSE, not to silence it for good.
#
# Rewritten for docs/design/intensity-gates.md. Lanes are the one piece of
# the old four that is GONE, and the reason is structural rather than a
# local fix: starvation was a fact about ONE rule winning ONE selection per
# tick, and there is no selection any more -- every rule whose gates are on
# fires, every tick, whatever else also fires. `<trip>`/`<cooldown-tick>`/
# `<revive>` do not need a guaranteed turn independent of the watched rule
# because nothing is competing with them for a turn to begin with. What
# stays, and why:
#
#   `dormant`/`due`   still the disable switch (`core/attention.py:_dormant`)
#                     -- unrelated to lanes, and the design doc's own task
#                     list does not retire it. A rule claimed dormant is
#                     skipped before matching is even attempted, this tick
#                     and every tick until something claims it `due`.
#   triggers          still see every SELECTION of a rule, even a no-op
#                     reselection -- `_intercept` runs inside `Machine.
#                     _pending`, before anything is committed.
#   a plain counter   the same computator-driven mutable-cell idiom as the
#                     purse transfer in `docs/guide.md`'s arithmetic
#                     chapter, and it is UNCHANGED: `tries`/`cooldown` are
#                     rewritten by erasing the old numeral-tagged fact and
#                     writing a new one, never by an intensity write on one
#                     node, so consumption's "same node, same tick, take
#                     the max" has nothing to do with how this counts.
#   `keep`            new, and the reason a fourth of this file's lines
#                     changed: firing discharges what it matches by
#                     default now (docs/design/intensity-gates.md), and a
#                     registration fact (`watched`, `threshold`,
#                     `cooldown_len`) or a counter read alongside its OWN
#                     explicit rewrite (`tries`, `cooldown`, `dormant_tag`)
#                     both need to survive being read -- see each rule
#                     below for which and why.
#
# Why these are load-bearing, found by testing rather than reasoning:
#
#   SUSPENSION IS THE GATE CLOSING, not `dormant(<R>)`. The breaker used
#   to name the watched RULE (`watched(<R>, tag)`, `+dormant($r)`), and a
#   rule reference is TAINTED: a rule's reification structurally contains
#   its own antecedent/consequent variables, `has_var` propagates, and a
#   variable bound to a rule could not be written into a new fact or
#   guarded with `no dormant($r)` at all. Routing everything through a
#   plain TAG was the workaround. With a gate there is no rule reference
#   left to taint -- `gate($tag)`, `beat($tag)`, `tries($tag, $n)` are all
#   ordinary ground facts, and the watched rule names the tag itself.
#
#   THE COOLDOWN CANNOT BE MEASURED BY THE WATCHED RULE'S OWN ACTIVITY,
#   because once its gate is shut it beats nothing to count.
#   `<cooldown-tick>` counts TICKS instead, firing every tick
#   `cooling($tag)` holds regardless of whether the watched rule is doing
#   anything at all.
#
# To watch a rule: give it `keep gate(a-tag)` in its antecedent and
# `+beat(a-tag)` in its consequent, then
# `fact +gate(a-tag)`, `fact +tries(a-tag, 0)`,
# `fact +threshold(a-tag, N)`, `fact +cooldown_len(a-tag, ROUNDS)`.
# Everything below is shared by every watched rule.

# Counts the watched rule's own BEATS. This used to be a write-time
# trigger reading `producing($r, $c)` -- the engine offering every pending
# conclusion to an interceptor before it committed -- and triggers are
# retired. A beat is the watched rule saying so itself: `+beat($tag)` in
# its consequent, one line, alongside the `keep gate($tag)` that lets the
# breaker suspend it. That is the cost of watching a rule now, and it is
# visible in the rule being watched rather than hidden in a hook.
rule <watchdog> = implies(
    { +beat($tag), keep tries($tag, $n), plus($n, 1) as $n2 },
    { -tries($tag, $n), +tries($tag, $n2) } )

# `keep` on `threshold` and `cooldown_len`: registration facts a corpus
# writes ONCE (`fact +threshold(tag, N)`, and so on) and
# this rule reads every time it considers tripping -- plain `+` would
# spend the registration itself on the FIRST trip, and there would be
# nothing left to read on the second. `tries` is also `keep` here: this
# rule only READS the count to compare it against the threshold; deciding
# a new one is `<watchdog>`'s job, above, and a plain `+tries($tag, $n)`
# here would race that rewrite -- two rules spending the SAME occasion in
# one tick over something that is supposed to persist.
rule <trip> = implies(
    { keep tries($tag, $n), keep threshold($tag, $max),
      at_least($n, $max) as $over, keep cooldown_len($tag, $len),
      +gate($tag), no cooling($tag) },
    { +cooling($tag), +cooldown($tag, $len) } )

# `keep cooling($tag)`: read every tick cooldown is counting down, so a
# plain `+` would spend the "we are cooling down" flag on the very first
# tick and the countdown would never see a second one. `cooldown($tag, $c)`
# itself needs no `keep` -- this rule's own `-cooldown($tag, $c)` erases
# the exact occasion it just matched, so default consumption and the
# explicit erase agree on the same target rather than racing it.
rule <cooldown-tick> = implies(
    { keep cooling($tag), +cooldown($tag, $c), at_least($c, 1) as $more, minus($c, 1) as $c2 },
    { -cooldown($tag, $c), +cooldown($tag, $c2) } )

# Once cooldown reaches 0: reactivate, AND reset the count -- so the rule
# gets a full, fresh budget rather than tripping again on its first move.
# `cooling`, `cooldown(_, 0)` and `tries($tag, $n)` all need no `keep` -- each is
# explicitly erased in the consequent below, at the exact node this rule
# matched, so consumption's implicit spend and the explicit one agree.
rule <revive> = implies(
    { +cooling($tag), +cooldown($tag, 0), +tries($tag, $n) },
    { +gate($tag), -tries($tag, $n), +tries($tag, 0), -cooldown($tag, 0) } )
